  "body_mappings": [
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        518.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "On Friday 5 December 1952, cold, stagnant air descended on London. Overhead, warmer air pressed down like a lid, holding the chill captive beneath. Londoners were accustomed to murky, dreary winters. This time, though, the capital's smoke was trapped too, thickening into a choking smog, a mix of smoke and fog."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        410.9614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "That smoke, from coal burned in domestic fireplaces and power stations, was laden with soot and noxious sulphur dioxide. In places, visibility fell to a few metres. Ambulance services were suspended, an opera at the Sadler's Wells theatre was abandoned at the interval, and prize cattle at the Smithfield agricultural show died or had to be slaughtered."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        303.4614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        402.4614173228347
      ],
      "text": "The smog dissipated on Tuesday 9 December. Officials at the time put the excess deaths at about 4,000, but a 1954 Ministry of Health report blamed later deaths on influenza. A study published in Environmental Health Perspectives in 2001 reassessed the evidence and cast doubt on that explanation, estimating about 12,000 excess deaths between December 1952 and February 1953."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        215.7614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        294.9614173228347
      ],
      "text": "For decades Londoners had tolerated the smoke as a nuisance. One villain flickered in their own grates, a source of warmth and comfort so habitual that few thought to accuse it. Such complacency may help to explain the government's grudging response. It withstood pressure for an inquiry until May 1953."
    },
    {
      "section": "en",
      "page": 2,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        108.26141732283469,
        461.8976377952756,
        207.2614173228347
      ],
      "text": "Reform proved protracted, slowed by official inertia. The Clean Air Act became law on 5 July 1956, three and a half years after the smog. It empowered municipal councils to create smokeless zones, combating smoke at its source. The cosy hearth, beloved of British families, had, all along, been among the chief offenders."
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        517.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        597.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "1952年12月5日星期五，寒冷、凝滞的空气笼罩降临了伦敦。头顶上方，较暖的空气压了下来，像盖子一样，把寒气困在下面。伦敦人早已习惯昏暗的、阴沉的冬天。不过这一次，首都的煤烟也被困住了，越积越浓，成了令人窒息的烟雾，也就是烟与雾的混合物。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        429.1614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        509.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "这些煤烟来自于烧的煤，在家庭壁炉和发电厂里，充满了煤烟和有毒的二氧化硫。有些地方，能见度降到只有几米。救护车服务被暂停，萨德勒之井剧院的一场歌剧在幕间休息时被中止，而史密斯菲尔德农业展上的获奖牛则死亡或不得不被宰杀。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        340.6614173228347,
        461.8976377952756,
        420.6614173228347
      ],
      "text": "烟雾消散于12月9日，星期二。当时的官员估计超额死亡约4,000人，但卫生部1954年的一份报告把后来的死亡归因于流感。2001年发表于《环境健康展望》的一项研究重新评估了证据，并对这一解释提出了质疑，估计在1952年12月至1953年2月期间约有12,000例超额死亡。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        252.16141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        332.1614173228347
      ],
      "text": "几十年来，伦敦人一直容忍着煤烟，只是把它当成一种让人讨厌的事情。一个元凶就在他们自家的炉栅里闪烁，一个温暖与舒适的来源，如此习以为常，以至于很少有人想到去指责它。这种自满，或许有助于解释政府勉强的回应。政府顶住了要求调查的压力，直到1953年的5月。"
    },
    {
      "section": "zh",
      "page": 3,
      "bbox": [
        37.0,
        163.66141732283472,
        461.8976377952756,
        243.66141732283472
      ],
      "text": "改革被证明是旷日持久的，并被官方的惰性拖慢了。《清洁空气法》于1956年7月5日成为法律，此时距那场烟雾已过去三年半。该法授权市镇议会设立无烟区，从源头上遏制煤烟。英国家庭深受喜爱的、温馨的炉膛，原来一直都在主要的肇事者之列。"
    }
  ],
